}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,5,0\\,).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1069 "q-328252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-7)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{20}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc46d129-8e8d-5162-b79e-c5e71e23455d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506588 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-7)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{20}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=7$ and $k=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,7,-2\\,).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1069 "q-328265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+9)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{100}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0b65e0b-a178-5086-8e32-4f470fc52a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506594 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-9))^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{100}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-9$ and $k=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,-9,-3\\,).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506599 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+18y=39?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec84be9a-d1c8-5ef5-8c9c-eaa63d5e60df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506599 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+18y & =39 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3[{y}^{2}-6y] & =39 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3[(y-3)^{2}-9] & =39 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3(y-3)^{2}+27 & =39 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3(y-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(0,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
